    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-13030",
      "problem": "Represent $x < -1$ on a number line.",
      "worked_solution": "The symbol \"$<$\" means \"less than.\"  So, the inequality $x < -1$ represents the numbers that are less than $-1.$\n\nOn a number line, the numbers less than $-1$ are the numbers that are left of  $-1.$ So, we should draw an arrow going to the left of $-1.$\n\nHowever, the number $-1$ itself is not less than $-1,$ so we should not include it in our representation. To exclude $-1,$ we draw an empty circle around $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14149933/assets/57499770d310c5f0912e62ec160accd878a1c169c22ce894a48102a39769d209.png)",
